    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Fiscal policy</span> is one of the two major tools (alongside monetary policy) that a government uses to manage the economy. It involves deliberate decisions by government about its <span class="learn-keyword">spending</span> and <span class="learn-keyword">taxation</span> in order to influence the level of economic activity, employment, prices, and growth.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Fiscal Policy</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Fiscal policy refers to the use of government <strong>revenue (taxation) and expenditure</strong> as instruments to achieve desired macroeconomic goals such as full employment, price stability, economic growth, and a favourable balance of payments. It is deliberately planned and implemented by the government, usually through the Ministry of Finance and the annual budget.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Tools (Instruments) of Fiscal Policy</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Taxation</td><td>Government can raise or lower tax rates (income tax, company tax, VAT) to reduce or increase disposable income and spending</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Government Expenditure</td><td>Government can increase spending on capital projects and services to stimulate the economy, or cut spending to reduce demand</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Public/Government Borrowing</td><td>Government borrows domestically or externally to finance a deficit budget when revenue is insufficient</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Transfer Payments</td><td>Subsidies, pensions, and grants used to redistribute income and influence spending power</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Types of Fiscal Policy</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Expansionary (reflationary) fiscal policy</strong> involves increasing government spending and/or cutting taxes to boost aggregate demand — used during a recession or depression. <strong>Contractionary (deflationary) fiscal policy</strong> involves reducing government spending and/or raising taxes to reduce aggregate demand — used to control inflation or an overheating economy.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Objectives of Fiscal Policy</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Fiscal policy aims to achieve: full employment; price stability (control of inflation/deflation); economic growth and development; equitable distribution of income and wealth; and a favourable balance of payments position.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Limitations of Fiscal Policy</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Fiscal policy in developing countries like Nigeria faces limitations including: time lags between decision and implementation; poor tax administration and low tax compliance; over-dependence on oil revenue; corruption and leakages in public expenditure; political interference; and the large size of the informal sector which is difficult to tax.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Do not confuse fiscal policy with monetary policy. Fiscal policy is about government taxation and spending (controlled by the Ministry of Finance/government), while monetary policy is about money supply and interest rates (controlled by the Central Bank).\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Fiscal policy is government\'s use of taxation and expenditure to manage the economy. Its main tools are taxation, government spending, borrowing, and transfer payments. It can be expansionary or contractionary depending on economic conditions, but is limited by administrative and structural weaknesses in developing economies.\n</div>\n',
