      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">National income accounting</span> measures the total value of goods and services produced in an economy over a given period. It provides key indicators — GDP, GNP, NNP, NI, PI, DI — used to assess economic performance and living standards.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Key Concepts</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>GDP (Gross Domestic Product)</td><td>Total value of goods and services produced within a country\'s borders in a year, regardless of who owns the resources</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>GNP (Gross National Product)</td><td>GDP plus net income from abroad (income earned by citizens abroad minus income earned by foreigners domestically)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>NNP (Net National Product)</td><td>GNP minus depreciation (capital consumption allowance)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>NI (National Income)</td><td>NNP minus indirect taxes plus subsidies (i.e., income measured at factor cost)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>PI (Personal Income)</td><td>National income minus undistributed profits, corporate taxes, and social security contributions, plus transfer payments</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>DI (Disposable Income)</td><td>Personal income minus personal taxes — the income actually available for spending or saving</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Methods of Measuring National Income</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Income method:</strong> sums all incomes (wages, rent, interest, profit) earned by factors of production. <strong>Output (product) method:</strong> sums the value added by all sectors of the economy. <strong>Expenditure method:</strong> sums all spending on final goods and services (consumption + investment + government spending + net exports).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Uses of National Income Data</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">National income statistics help to: measure a country\'s economic growth over time; compare living standards between countries; guide government economic planning and policy; and assess the structure and performance of different sectors of the economy.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Limitations of National Income Data</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">National income figures may not fully capture welfare because they: exclude non-market and informal sector activities (common in Nigeria); do not account for income distribution/inequality; ignore environmental degradation costs; and can be distorted by inaccurate data collection.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember the flow: GDP → (+ net income from abroad) → GNP → (− depreciation) → NNP → (− indirect tax + subsidy) → NI → (− corporate items + transfers) → PI → (− personal tax) → DI.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> National income accounting measures a country\'s economic output using concepts like GDP, GNP, NNP, NI, PI, and DI, calculated via the income, output, or expenditure methods. While useful for planning and comparison, the data has notable limitations, especially in economies with large informal sectors.\n</div>\n',
