    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Money</span> is anything generally accepted as a medium of exchange. Understanding why people want to hold money (<span class="learn-keyword">demand for money</span>) and how much money is available in the economy (<span class="learn-keyword">supply of money</span>) is central to monetary economics.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Definition of Money</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Money is any generally acceptable medium of exchange used to settle transactions, measure value, and store wealth. It performs four main functions: medium of exchange, measure/unit of value, store of value, and standard for deferred payment.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Demand for Money — The Motives</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Motive</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Transactionary (Transactions) Motive</td><td>Holding money to meet everyday spending needs — buying goods and services</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Precautionary Motive</td><td>Holding money to cover unexpected expenses or emergencies</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Speculative Motive</td><td>Holding money to take advantage of expected future changes in interest rates or asset prices (rather than investing immediately)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<p class="learn-p">This classification, developed by John Maynard Keynes, explains why individuals and businesses choose to hold liquid cash rather than investing all their funds in interest-earning assets.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Supply of Money</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The money supply is the total stock of money circulating in an economy at a given time. It is usually measured as narrow money (M1: currency in circulation + demand deposits) and broad money (M2: M1 + savings and time deposits).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Determinants of Money Supply</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Money supply is influenced by: the Central Bank\'s monetary policy (interest rates, reserve requirements, open market operations); the level of commercial bank lending/credit creation; government fiscal deficits financed by money creation; and the balance of payments position (inflows/outflows of foreign exchange).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember the three motives with the mnemonic "TPS" — Transactionary, Precautionary, Speculative. The speculative motive is the one most sensitive to changes in interest rates.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Money is demanded for transactionary, precautionary, and speculative reasons, while its supply is controlled largely by the Central Bank through monetary policy tools, along with commercial bank credit creation and government financing needs.\n</div>\n',
