    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Inflation</span> and <span class="learn-keyword">deflation</span> describe persistent changes in the general price level of an economy. Both affect the value of money, purchasing power, and overall economic stability.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Inflation</strong> is a persistent (sustained) rise in the general price level of goods and services over time, resulting in a fall in the purchasing power of money. <strong>Deflation</strong> is a persistent fall in the general price level, resulting in a rise in the purchasing power of money.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Types of Inflation</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Cause</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Demand-Pull Inflation</td><td>Too much money chasing too few goods — aggregate demand exceeds aggregate supply</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Cost-Push Inflation</td><td>Rising production costs (wages, raw materials, energy) pushed on to consumers as higher prices</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Structural Inflation</td><td>Caused by structural weaknesses such as poor infrastructure and inefficient production systems, common in developing economies</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Imported Inflation</td><td>Caused by rising prices of imported goods, especially where a country depends heavily on imports</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Causes and Effects of Inflation</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Causes:</strong> excess money supply, high government spending, rising costs of production, currency devaluation, and supply shortages. <strong>Effects:</strong> reduced purchasing power, redistribution of income (hurts fixed-income earners, benefits debtors), discourages savings, may boost exports\' competitiveness if a country\'s inflation is lower than trading partners, and can destabilize planning.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Causes and Effects of Deflation</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Causes:</strong> falling money supply, reduced aggregate demand, and economic recession. <strong>Effects:</strong> increases the real value of debt (harms borrowers), can lead to reduced production and rising unemployment as firms cut back due to falling prices and profits, and may trigger a deflationary spiral.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Control Measures</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Inflation is controlled through <strong>contractionary monetary policy</strong> (raising interest rates, reducing money supply) and <strong>contractionary fiscal policy</strong> (raising taxes, cutting government spending). Deflation is controlled through <strong>expansionary monetary policy</strong> (lowering interest rates, increasing money supply) and <strong>expansionary fiscal policy</strong> (tax cuts, increased government spending).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Demand-pull inflation is caused by excess demand; cost-push inflation is caused by rising costs of production. Examiners frequently test the difference using real-world examples like fuel price hikes (cost-push) versus festive-season spending surges (demand-pull).\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Inflation is a sustained rise in prices while deflation is a sustained fall. Both distort economic activity but for different reasons and are controlled using opposite (expansionary vs. contractionary) monetary and fiscal policy measures.\n</div>\n',
