    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Studying how other economies achieved rapid <span class="learn-keyword">growth and development</span> provides valuable lessons for Nigeria and other developing countries. The <span class="learn-keyword">Asian Tigers</span>, Japan, Europe, and America each followed distinct paths to economic success.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. The Asian Tigers</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The "Asian Tigers" — South Korea, Taiwan, Singapore, and Hong Kong — transformed from poor, agrarian economies into industrialized, high-income economies within a few decades through export-oriented industrialization, heavy investment in education, strong government-guided planning, and openness to foreign trade and technology.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Key Growth Strategies</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Economy</th><th>Key Strategy</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Asian Tigers</td><td>Export-led industrialization, investment in human capital, disciplined savings and investment</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Japan</td><td>Post-war reconstruction, technological innovation, strong work ethic, quality manufacturing (e.g., automobiles, electronics)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Europe</td><td>Regional economic integration (EU), industrial diversification, strong institutions and social welfare systems</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>America (USA)</td><td>Free-market capitalism, innovation and entrepreneurship, strong property rights, large domestic market</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Common Success Factors</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Despite different approaches, these economies share common growth drivers: heavy investment in education and skills development; political and economic stability; strong institutions with rule of law; investment in infrastructure; openness to trade and foreign investment; and a focus on technology and innovation.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Lessons Nigeria Can Learn</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Nigeria can learn to: diversify away from oil dependence into manufacturing and services; invest heavily in education and technical skills; build strong, stable institutions to fight corruption; encourage export-oriented industries; and create a stable, predictable environment for both local and foreign investment.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Examiners often ask candidates to state specific lessons Nigeria can learn from these economies — always tie your answer back to Nigeria\'s context (e.g., diversifying from oil, like the Asian Tigers diversified from agriculture).\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> The Asian Tigers, Japan, Europe, and America achieved rapid growth through education, industrialization, stable institutions, and trade openness. Nigeria can apply similar lessons by diversifying its economy, investing in human capital, and strengthening institutions.\n</div>\n',
