    topic: 'Human Capital Development',
    topicCode: 'SS2-ECO-07',
    module: 'Economic Development',
    term: 'First Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Human capital</span> refers to the knowledge, skills, health, and abilities embodied in people that make them productive. Investing in human capital is one of the most important drivers of long-term economic growth and development.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Human Capital Development</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Human capital development refers to the process of improving the skills, knowledge, health, and productive capacities of a population through education, training, healthcare, and other investments in people. It transforms raw labour into skilled, productive human resources.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Importance of Human Capital Development</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Benefit</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Increased Productivity</td><td>Skilled and educated workers produce more output with the same resources</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Innovation</td><td>Educated populations generate more ideas, inventions, and technological advancement</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Higher Incomes</td><td>Skilled workers command higher wages, improving living standards</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Economic Diversification</td><td>A skilled workforce enables growth of varied sectors beyond primary production</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Attracts Investment</td><td>Investors are drawn to countries with skilled, employable labour forces</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Problems of Human Capital Development in Nigeria</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Nigeria faces several challenges including: inadequate funding for education and healthcare; poor quality of educational infrastructure and outdated curricula; brain drain (skilled professionals emigrating abroad); high rates of out-of-school children; mismatch between skills produced and labour market needs; and poor healthcare access affecting workforce productivity.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Solutions to Human Capital Development Problems</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Possible solutions include: increasing government investment in education and healthcare; reforming curricula to match modern labour market needs (especially technical/vocational skills); improving healthcare access and quality; creating incentives to retain skilled professionals; and public-private partnerships to fund training programs.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> "Brain drain" is a favourite exam term — it refers to the emigration of highly skilled and educated professionals (doctors, engineers, academics) from a country, which represents a loss of that country\'s human capital investment.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Human capital development builds a productive, skilled workforce essential for economic growth. Nigeria faces challenges like underfunding, brain drain, and skills mismatch, which can be addressed through increased investment in education, healthcare, and targeted policy reforms.\n</div>\n',
