    topic: 'Petroleum and the Nigerian Economy',
    topicCode: 'SS2-ECO-08',
    module: 'Nigerian Economy',
    term: 'First Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Petroleum (crude oil)</span> has played a dominant role in Nigeria\'s economy since its discovery, shaping government revenue, foreign exchange earnings, and national development — but also creating significant economic challenges.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. History of Petroleum in Nigeria</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Crude oil was discovered in commercial quantities at Oloibiri, in present-day Bayelsa State, in 1956 by Shell-BP. Nigeria became a major oil exporter by the 1970s, and petroleum quickly overtook agriculture as the country\'s dominant revenue and foreign exchange earner.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Contributions of Petroleum to the Nigerian Economy</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Contribution</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Government Revenue</td><td>Oil sales account for a large share of federal government revenue</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Foreign Exchange Earnings</td><td>Crude oil exports are Nigeria\'s largest source of foreign exchange</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>GDP Contribution</td><td>The oil sector contributes significantly to national output, though less than the non-oil sector in recent years</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Employment</td><td>Direct and indirect jobs in exploration, production, and related services</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Infrastructure Development</td><td>Oil revenue has funded major infrastructure projects across the country</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Problems Associated with Oil Dependence</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Nigeria\'s heavy dependence on oil has led to: <strong>the "resource curse"</strong> — neglect of other productive sectors like agriculture and manufacturing; vulnerability to global oil price volatility, which destabilizes government revenue and the naira; environmental degradation and pollution in oil-producing communities (especially the Niger Delta); corruption and mismanagement of oil revenue; and social unrest/conflict in oil-producing regions.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Diversification Strategies</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">To reduce oil dependence, Nigeria has pursued strategies including: promoting agriculture and agro-allied industries; developing the solid minerals sector; boosting manufacturing and industrialization; growing the services sector (ICT, entertainment, finance); improving the ease of doing business to attract non-oil investment; and building local refining capacity to add value to crude oil.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> The term "resource curse" (or "paradox of plenty") is a key exam concept — it describes how countries rich in natural resources like oil can experience slower overall growth and development due to over-reliance on that single resource, neglecting other sectors.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Petroleum has been central to Nigeria\'s revenue and foreign exchange earnings since the 1950s/60s, but over-dependence has created vulnerability to price shocks, environmental problems, and neglect of other sectors — making diversification a key economic priority.\n</div>\n',
