    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  This topic continues the study of Nigeria\'s financial regulatory framework, focusing on the <span class="learn-keyword">Nigerian Exchange (NGX/NSE)</span>, the <span class="learn-keyword">National Insurance Commission (NAICOM)</span>, and the <span class="learn-keyword">Federal Ministry of Finance</span>.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. The Nigerian Exchange (NGX, formerly NSE)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The Nigerian Exchange provides a formal, organized platform where shares, bonds, and other securities of listed companies are bought and sold. Its functions include: providing liquidity for investors to buy and sell securities; mobilizing long-term savings for productive investment; providing a price discovery mechanism for securities; and enforcing listing rules and disclosure standards for companies quoted on the exchange.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. National Insurance Commission (NAICOM)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">NAICOM regulates the insurance industry in Nigeria. Its functions include: licensing and supervising insurance and reinsurance companies; ensuring insurance companies remain solvent and can meet claims; protecting policyholders\' interests; approving insurance products and premium rates; and promoting the growth and development of the insurance industry.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Federal Ministry of Finance</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The Ministry of Finance is responsible for: formulating and coordinating national fiscal policy; preparing and managing the national budget; overseeing government revenue collection and public expenditure; managing public debt; and coordinating with other regulatory agencies (CBN, SEC, NAICOM) on overall economic policy.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Comparative Roles</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Agency</th><th>Primary Focus</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Nigerian Exchange (NGX)</td><td>Trading platform for shares and securities</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>NAICOM</td><td>Regulation of insurance companies and policyholder protection</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Ministry of Finance</td><td>Fiscal policy, budget, and overall economic policy coordination</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Note the distinction between SEC and NGX: SEC is the regulator (sets rules and oversees the market), while NGX is the actual trading platform/marketplace where securities are bought and sold, operating under SEC\'s supervision.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> The Nigerian Exchange provides the platform for trading securities, NAICOM regulates and protects the insurance industry and policyholders, and the Ministry of Finance coordinates national fiscal policy — completing Nigeria\'s multi-agency financial regulatory framework.\n</div>\n',
