    topic: 'International Trade',
    topicCode: 'SS2-ECO-13',
    module: 'International Economics',
    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">International trade</span> is the exchange of goods and services across national borders. It allows countries to specialize in what they produce best and benefit from exchange, but also presents challenges that must be carefully managed.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of International Trade</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">International trade refers to the buying and selling of goods and services between residents of different countries. It arises because countries differ in resource endowments, climate, technology, and labour costs, making it beneficial to specialize and exchange.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Types of International Trade</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Bilateral Trade</td><td>Trade between two countries</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Multilateral Trade</td><td>Trade among three or more countries, often under trade agreements</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Import Trade</td><td>Buying goods and services from other countries</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Export Trade</td><td>Selling goods and services to other countries</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Entrepôt Trade</td><td>Importing goods for re-export to a third country, often after minor processing</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Importance of International Trade</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">International trade allows countries to: access goods and resources not available domestically; earn foreign exchange through exports; benefit from specialization according to comparative advantage; access larger markets for domestic producers; and gain access to new technology and ideas.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Theories of Trade: Absolute and Comparative Advantage</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Absolute advantage</strong> (Adam Smith) occurs when a country can produce a good using fewer resources than another country. <strong>Comparative advantage</strong> (David Ricardo) states that even if one country is more efficient at producing everything, both countries can still gain from trade if each specializes in producing the good where it has the lowest opportunity cost.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Problems of International Trade</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Challenges include: trade imbalances (persistent deficits); exposure to global price volatility; dependence on foreign technology and capital goods; trade barriers such as tariffs and quotas imposed by trading partners; and exchange rate fluctuations affecting the value of trade.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Comparative advantage is the most frequently examined theory — remember it is based on opportunity cost, not just absolute efficiency. A country should specialize in what it gives up the least to produce.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> International trade allows countries to specialize and exchange based on absolute or comparative advantage, generating foreign exchange and access to goods and technology, but it also carries risks like trade imbalances and exposure to global price volatility.\n</div>\n',
