    topic: 'Economic Growth and Development',
    topicCode: 'SS2-ECO-14',
    module: 'Economic Development',
    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Although often used interchangeably, <span class="learn-keyword">economic growth</span> and <span class="learn-keyword">economic development</span> are distinct concepts. Understanding the difference is essential for evaluating a country\'s true progress.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning and Distinction</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Economic growth</strong> refers to a quantitative increase in a country\'s output of goods and services (usually measured by rising GDP or GNP) over time. <strong>Economic development</strong> is a broader, qualitative concept that includes growth plus improvements in living standards, income distribution, education, health, infrastructure, and overall quality of life.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Economic Growth</th><th>Economic Development</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Quantitative (measurable increase in output)</td><td>Qualitative and quantitative (broader improvement in welfare)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Measured by GDP/GNP growth rate</td><td>Measured by Human Development Index (HDI), literacy, life expectancy, poverty rates</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Can occur without improving living standards for all</td><td>Implies improved living standards and reduced inequality</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>A narrower concept</td><td>A broader, more comprehensive concept — growth is a component of development</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Indicators of Economic Development</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Key indicators include: GDP/GNP per capita; literacy and education rates; life expectancy and health outcomes; poverty and unemployment rates; income distribution/inequality (e.g., Gini coefficient); access to infrastructure (electricity, water, roads); and the Human Development Index (HDI), which combines income, education, and health.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Problems of Achieving Growth and Development</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Challenges include: political instability and weak governance; inadequate infrastructure; low levels of savings and investment; corruption; over-dependence on a narrow resource base (e.g., oil); poor education and health outcomes; and rapid population growth outpacing economic gains.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Strategies for Promoting Growth and Development</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Strategies include: investing in infrastructure and human capital; promoting economic diversification; encouraging savings and productive investment; strengthening institutions and reducing corruption; implementing sound fiscal and monetary policy; and pursuing inclusive policies that reduce inequality.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> A common exam question asks candidates to explain why a country can experience growth without development. The key answer: GDP can rise due to a booming sector (e.g., oil) while poverty, inequality, and poor living standards for the majority persist unchanged.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Economic growth is a quantitative rise in output, while economic development is a broader improvement in overall living standards and welfare. Sustainable progress requires policies that promote both growth and genuine, inclusive development.\n</div>\n',
