    topic: 'Balance of Payments (BOP)',
    topicCode: 'SS2-ECO-15',
    module: 'International Economics',
    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  The <span class="learn-keyword">balance of payments (BOP)</span> is a systematic record of all economic transactions between a country\'s residents and the rest of the world over a given period, typically one year.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Balance of Payments</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The balance of payments is a statistical statement that records all transactions between residents of a country and the rest of the world, including trade in goods and services, income flows, and capital transfers, over a specific period (usually a year).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Components of the Balance of Payments</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Account</th><th>Records</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Current Account</td><td>Trade in goods (visible) and services (invisible), income flows, and current transfers</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Capital Account</td><td>Transfers of capital, such as debt forgiveness and migrants\' transfers</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Financial Account</td><td>Foreign direct investment, portfolio investment, and other financial flows</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Official Reserves Account</td><td>Changes in a country\'s foreign exchange reserves held by the central bank</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Causes of Disequilibrium (Imbalance)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A balance of payments disequilibrium (deficit or surplus) can be caused by: structural changes in trade patterns; excessive imports relative to exports; over-valuation or under-valuation of the domestic currency; large capital outflows or inflows; inflation making exports less competitive; and heavy dependence on a narrow range of exports vulnerable to price shocks.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Measures for Correcting BOP Problems</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Correcting a BOP deficit may involve: currency devaluation/depreciation to make exports cheaper and imports costlier; import restrictions (tariffs, quotas) to reduce import spending; export promotion policies to boost foreign exchange earnings; deflationary (contractionary) monetary and fiscal policy to reduce domestic demand for imports; and attracting foreign investment to boost the capital/financial account.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Do not confuse "balance of trade" (just visible goods) with "balance of payments" (the full record including goods, services, income, and capital flows). BOP is the broader, more comprehensive concept.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> The balance of payments records all of a country\'s economic transactions with the rest of the world across current, capital, financial, and reserves accounts. Disequilibrium can arise from trade imbalances or currency issues, and is typically corrected through devaluation, trade restrictions, export promotion, or demand-reducing policies.\n</div>\n',
