    term: 'Third Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Economic development planning</span> is the deliberate, systematic effort by government to direct and coordinate a country\'s economic resources towards achieving specific development goals over a defined period.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Economic Planning</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Economic planning is the process by which government deliberately sets economic objectives and coordinates the use of resources — through policies, budgets, and programmes — to achieve desired growth and development outcomes over a specific period.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Objectives of Economic Planning</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Economic plans typically aim to: accelerate the rate of economic growth; diversify the economic base; reduce unemployment and poverty; improve infrastructure; achieve balanced regional development; and promote self-reliance and reduce dependence on foreign aid/imports.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Types of Plans</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Duration</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Short-term Plan</td><td>1–2 years, addresses immediate/urgent needs</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Medium-term Plan</td><td>3–5 years, common for national development plans</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Long-term Plan (Perspective Plan)</td><td>10–25 years or more, sets broad long-range vision and direction</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Problems of Economic Planning in Nigeria</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Nigeria\'s planning efforts have historically faced challenges including: inadequate and unreliable statistical/data systems for planning; poor implementation and lack of political will; frequent policy discontinuity with changes in government; corruption and diversion of planned resources; over-ambitious targets not matched by available resources; and inadequate involvement of the private sector and citizens in plan design.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> "Policy discontinuity" is a frequently tested term — it refers to the tendency for new governments in Nigeria to abandon or drastically alter previous administrations\' development plans, undermining long-term planning consistency.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Economic planning coordinates a country\'s resources toward growth and development goals through short, medium, or long-term plans. In Nigeria, planning has been hampered by poor data, weak implementation, policy discontinuity, and corruption.\n</div>\n',
