    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">International economic organizations</span> such as the <span class="learn-keyword">IMF</span> and <span class="learn-keyword">World Bank</span> were established to promote global economic stability, provide financial assistance, and support development, especially in poorer nations.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. International Monetary Fund (IMF)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The IMF was established in 1944 to promote international monetary cooperation, exchange rate stability, and orderly international trade. Its objectives include: promoting global monetary cooperation; providing short-term financial assistance to countries facing balance of payments difficulties; monitoring and advising on members\' economic and financial policies; and promoting exchange rate stability.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. World Bank</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The World Bank was also established in 1944, alongside the IMF, but focuses on long-term development financing. Its objectives include: providing long-term loans and grants for development projects (infrastructure, education, health); reducing global poverty; supporting economic reforms in developing countries; and providing technical assistance and policy advice.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Key Differences Between IMF and World Bank</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>IMF</th><th>World Bank</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Focus</td><td>Short-term monetary/BOP stability</td><td>Long-term development financing</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Loan Type</td><td>Short-term loans to correct BOP problems</td><td>Long-term project and development loans</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Main Concern</td><td>Global financial and exchange rate stability</td><td>Poverty reduction and development projects</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Benefits to Member Countries</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Both institutions offer benefits including: access to emergency/development financing; technical expertise and policy advice; a platform for international economic cooperation; and support during economic crises.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Criticisms</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Both organizations face criticism for: imposing strict conditionalities (such as Structural Adjustment Programmes) that can cause short-term hardship; being dominated by developed/Western countries in decision-making; sometimes prescribing one-size-fits-all policies that don\'t suit local conditions; and contributing to debt burdens in developing countries.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember the simple distinction: IMF = short-term monetary/BOP stability; World Bank = long-term development financing. Both were created at the Bretton Woods Conference of 1944.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> The IMF promotes monetary cooperation and provides short-term BOP assistance, while the World Bank provides long-term development financing to reduce poverty. Both offer significant benefits to member countries but are criticized for strict conditionalities and unequal influence in decision-making.\n</div>\n',
