    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Nigeria has adopted several national development frameworks over the years, including the <span class="learn-keyword">MDGs</span>, <span class="learn-keyword">NEEDS</span>, and <span class="learn-keyword">Vision 2020</span>, each with specific goals for national transformation.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Millennium Development Goals (MDGs)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The MDGs were eight global development goals adopted by United Nations member states (including Nigeria) in 2000, targeted for achievement by 2015. They covered: eradicating extreme poverty and hunger; achieving universal primary education; promoting gender equality; reducing child mortality; improving maternal health; combating HIV/AIDS, malaria, and other diseases; ensuring environmental sustainability; and developing a global partnership for development.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. National Economic Empowerment and Development Strategy (NEEDS)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">NEEDS was Nigeria\'s home-grown poverty reduction and economic reform programme launched in 2004. Its objectives included: wealth creation; employment generation; poverty reduction; and value re-orientation, pursued through macroeconomic stability, structural reforms, deregulation, and governance/institutional reforms. It had state-level (SEEDS) and local-level (LEEDS) counterparts.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Vision 2020 (Nigeria Vision 20:2020)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Vision 20:2020 was a long-term development blueprint aimed at making Nigeria one of the top 20 economies in the world by the year 2020, with a large, strong, diversified economy providing a high quality of life for citizens. It focused on developing critical sectors such as infrastructure, agriculture, manufacturing, and human capital.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Achievements, Challenges, and Evaluation</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Key Achievement</th><th>Key Challenge</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>MDGs</td><td>Progress on primary school enrollment and disease control in some areas</td><td>Nigeria fell short of most targets by the 2015 deadline</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>NEEDS</td><td>Contributed to macroeconomic stabilization and debt relief negotiations</td><td>Weak implementation and limited impact on poverty at grassroots level</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Vision 20:2020</td><td>Set ambitious, structured long-term targets for national transformation</td><td>Nigeria did not achieve top-20 economy status by 2020; plan was largely unrealized</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> A common exam theme is evaluating why these ambitious plans (MDGs, NEEDS, Vision 2020) largely fell short of their targets — key recurring reasons include weak implementation, inadequate funding, corruption, and policy discontinuity.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> The MDGs set global development targets for 2015, NEEDS was Nigeria\'s home-grown poverty reduction strategy from 2004, and Vision 20:2020 aimed to make Nigeria a top-20 global economy by 2020. All three had ambitious goals but faced significant implementation challenges.\n</div>\n',
