    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Economic reform programmes</span> such as the Structural Adjustment Programme (SAP), deregulation, privatization, and commercialization were introduced in Nigeria to restructure the economy and reduce excessive government control over economic activities.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Structural Adjustment Programme (SAP)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">SAP was introduced in Nigeria in 1986 (under IMF/World Bank influence) to restructure the economy. Its measures included: devaluation of the naira; removal of subsidies (especially fuel subsidies); trade liberalization (reducing import restrictions); privatization of state-owned enterprises; and reduction of public sector spending. Objectives were to reduce dependence on oil, encourage local production, and correct macroeconomic imbalances.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Deregulation</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Deregulation involves reducing or removing government controls and restrictions on economic activities (such as price controls, licensing requirements, and interest rate ceilings) to allow market forces to determine prices and resource allocation more freely, encouraging efficiency and competition.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Privatization and Commercialization</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Privatization</td><td>The transfer of ownership of government-owned enterprises to private individuals/companies</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Commercialization</td><td>Running government-owned enterprises on a profit-oriented, commercial basis while ownership remains with government</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Objectives of These Reforms</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">These programmes aimed to: reduce the burden of loss-making state enterprises on government finances; improve efficiency and service delivery through competition; attract private investment and reduce corruption in state enterprises; and correct macroeconomic imbalances (fiscal deficits, overvalued currency, trade imbalances).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Benefits and Problems</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><strong>Benefits:</strong> improved efficiency in previously loss-making enterprises (e.g., telecommunications after privatization); reduced fiscal burden on government; increased private investment and competition. <strong>Problems:</strong> job losses from restructuring/downsizing; increased cost of living from subsidy removal; social hardship, especially for the poor, in the short term; and cases of poorly managed or corrupt privatization processes benefiting a few individuals.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Distinguish privatization (ownership transfers to private hands) from commercialization (government retains ownership but the enterprise operates for profit). This distinction is frequently tested.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> SAP, deregulation, privatization, and commercialization were reform programmes aimed at restructuring Nigeria\'s economy, reducing government control, and improving efficiency. While they brought benefits like improved efficiency and reduced fiscal burden, they also caused short-term hardship such as job losses and higher living costs.\n</div>\n',
